---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';

const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/activecampaign-vs-klaviyo';
const title = 'ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo for Shopify Email: Pricing and Fit';
const desc = 'ActiveCampaign or Klaviyo for a Shopify store? Compare the pricing units, the data model, the sales-pipeline question and the migration path.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is ActiveCampaign or Klaviyo the better choice for a Shopify store?',
    answer: 'For most Shopify stores that want email and SMS built around customer purchases and browsing, Klaviyo is the better choice. ActiveCampaign is the better choice when the same contact list also drives a sales pipeline, lead scoring or nurture on a content site.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do the prices compare?',
    answer: 'ActiveCampaign lists its Starter plan from $15 a month for 1,000 contacts, and the Plus plan from $49 a month at the same size. Klaviyo lists its email plans from $20 a month for 500 profiles, $30 for 1,000 and $60 for 2,500. The units are different (contacts versus active profiles), so compare both at the same list size on each vendor’s pricing page.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Klaviyo have a free plan, and does ActiveCampaign?',
    answer: 'Klaviyo has a free plan that covers up to 250 active profiles and 500 email sends a month. ActiveCampaign does not list an ongoing free plan, but it offers a free trial. Check the pricing page for the current trial length.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I move from ActiveCampaign to Klaviyo, or back?',
    answer: 'Yes. Export contacts together with their consent records and custom fields, rebuild the welcome and cart flows first, and run both platforms in parallel for a short period with suppression rules in place. CRM-specific parts of an ActiveCampaign setup, such as deal stages, usually need to be rebuilt as tags or segments in Klaviyo.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which tool is better for wholesale or B2B sales?',
    answer: 'ActiveCampaign is usually the better fit when wholesale inquiries need deal stages and sales follow-up. Klaviyo can tag and branch these contacts, but it is designed around consumer ecommerce rather than a sales pipeline.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which platform is better for sale periods and high send volume?',
    answer: 'Klaviyo is built around ecommerce segments such as recent purchasers, so suppression rules are easier to set up for sale events. With ActiveCampaign, the same suppression often depends on tags you maintain yourself. Either tool needs list hygiene and a tested send schedule before a big promotion.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should I test in a trial before choosing?',
    answer: 'Build a welcome flow, an abandoned cart flow and one post-purchase flow on your own store. Check how quickly new orders and events appear in each tool, whether you can exclude recent buyers, and how long it takes a non-technical team member to change a segment. Use the results together with the pricing at your real list size.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo for Shopify email</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-[#5d3a1d]">Written by the editorial team</p>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
          For most Shopify stores, Klaviyo is the better pick, because its data model is built around shopping behaviour and its free plan covers up to 250 active profiles. The starting prices are not directly comparable: Klaviyo’s email plans begin at $20 a month for 500 profiles, while ActiveCampaign’s Starter plan begins at $15 a month for 1,000 contacts. ActiveCampaign becomes the better pick when the same list also feeds a sales pipeline, lead scoring or nurture on a content site.
        </p>
        <!-- screenshot-unavailable: no licensed screenshots of either vendor dashboard are stored with this site -->
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Shopify retention', tool: 'Klaviyo', reason: 'Built around customer records and purchase behaviour, with a free plan for small lists.' },
            { label: 'Sales pipeline', tool: 'ActiveCampaign', reason: 'Deal stages and lead scoring sit alongside email automation.' },
            { label: 'Lowest entry price', tool: 'ActiveCampaign', reason: 'Starter plan from $15 a month at 1,000 contacts, billed monthly or annually.' },
            { label: 'Email plus SMS in one list', tool: 'Klaviyo', reason: 'SMS sits in the same profile model, with pricing on request.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Best for</th><th>Starting price</th><th>Main limitation</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>ActiveCampaign</td>
              <td>Shops whose email list also feeds sales deals or content nurture</td>
              <td>$15 a month, Starter plan, 1,000 contacts (monthly or annual billing)</td>
              <td>Shopify-specific triggers are not the default, so setup takes more work</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Klaviyo</td>
              <td>Shopify stores focused on retention, with email and SMS</td>
              <td>Free for up to 250 active profiles; $20 a month for the 500-profile email plan</td>
              <td>Cost rises with profile count; Email+SMS plans are priced on request</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Why the choice matters for a Shopify store</h2>
        <p>
          Both tools connect to Shopify and can send welcome, cart and post-purchase emails, so the question is less about whether they work and more about what they are built around. Klaviyo organises contacts as profiles tied to store activity. ActiveCampaign organises contacts as a general CRM record that can be connected to a store. That difference shows up in the setup work, in how segments are built, and in what each platform costs as the list grows.
        </p>
        <p>
          If your revenue comes mostly from people buying products online, Klaviyo usually needs less custom setup to reach the flows that matter most. If your store also takes wholesale or B2B enquiries, or runs a blog that generates leads, ActiveCampaign can hold those contacts in one place, which is the case it was designed for.
        </p>

        <h2>ActiveCampaign: where it fits</h2>
        <p>
          ActiveCampaign is a marketing automation and CRM platform. Its strengths are deal stages, lead scoring, site tracking and automations that combine several sources of contact data. A store that sells to other businesses, runs membership upgrades or sends follow-up from a sales inbox will find those features useful.
        </p>
        <p>
          Its limitation for a Shopify-only shop is that store events are one input among many. Shopify-specific triggers are available, but you should expect to spend more time building and checking them than you would in a tool built around the store. Tags and custom fields become important because they carry the logic that a Shopify-native tool would hold by default.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Price:</strong> the Starter plan is listed at $15 a month for 1,000 contacts, the Plus plan at $49 and the Pro plan at $79, with Enterprise at $145 for the same starting size. Monthly and annual billing show the same per-month price on the pricing page. Check the current contact tiers on the <a class="link" href="https://www.activecampaign.com/pricing">ActiveCampaign pricing page</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Klaviyo: where it fits</h2>
        <p>
          Klaviyo is built for ecommerce email and SMS. Its profiles are linked to store activity, which makes it straightforward to build segments by purchase history, product interest and engagement, and to use those segments in flows. Email and SMS share one profile model, so a customer can be reached on either channel from the same segment.
        </p>
        <p>
          Its limitation is that it is not a CRM. Sales teams that need deal stages, pipeline views or lead scoring for wholesale will find it limited, and will likely need a separate tool for that work. Its cost also rises with the number of active profiles, so a large list that is not cleaned can become expensive quickly.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Price:</strong> the free plan covers up to 250 active profiles and 500 email sends a month, plus $5 of mobile messages. The email plans are listed at $20 a month for 500 profiles, $30 for 1,000, $60 for 2,500, $100 for 5,000, $150 for 10,000 and $400 for 25,000. Email+SMS plans are priced on request. See the <a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/pricing">Klaviyo pricing page</a> for the current figures.
        </p>

        <h2>Pricing at the same list size</h2>
        <p>
          The two vendors count different things, so a like-for-like comparison has to start from the list size you actually have. At 1,000 contacts or profiles, ActiveCampaign’s Starter plan is $15 a month and Klaviyo’s 1,000-profile email plan is $30 a month. At 2,500, ActiveCampaign’s Plus plan is $49 a month and Klaviyo’s 2,500-profile plan is $60. These are list-price figures from each vendor’s pricing page, and they do not include any feature add-ons or SMS.
        </p>
        <p>
          On an annual view, multiply the monthly price by 12. For example, 12 × $49 = $588 a year for ActiveCampaign’s Plus plan at its starting size, and 12 × $60 = $720 a year for Klaviyo’s 2,500-profile email plan. Those numbers show the base cost only. Add SMS, extra seats or higher feature tiers before you decide.
        </p>

        <h2>Migration steps if you switch</h2>
        <p>
          Moving between the two is manageable for a store with a clean list. Start by exporting contacts with their consent status, subscription source and custom fields. Confirm that the consent data came across before you import anything, because a missing consent flag is the most common reason a migration causes problems.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next, rebuild the welcome flow and the cart flow in the new tool and check each step with test orders. Run the old and new platforms side by side for a short period, with suppression rules so the same customer is not emailed twice. Finally, retire the old flows one at a time. CRM-specific steps such as deal stages usually need a different design in Klaviyo, often as tags or segments.
        </p>

        <h2>How to choose</h2>
        <p><strong>Choose Klaviyo if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Most of your revenue comes from online orders, and retention is the main goal</li>
          <li>You want email and SMS to share one set of customer profiles</li>
          <li>Your list is small enough to start on the free plan or the lower email tiers</li>
          <li>You do not need a sales pipeline in the same tool</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Choose ActiveCampaign if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Wholesale, B2B or membership enquiries need deal stages and lead scoring</li>
          <li>The same contacts receive content from a blog or site that you also track</li>
          <li>Your team already works in a CRM and wants marketing in the same place</li>
          <li>You are willing to build Shopify logic with tags and custom fields</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>A trial plan that answers the question</h2>
        <p>
          Use the free trial or the free plan to test with your own data. Build a welcome flow, an abandoned cart flow and one post-purchase flow. Check how quickly new orders and events appear in each tool, and whether a recent buyer can be excluded from a promotion without a manual list edit.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then ask someone who will run the account day to day to change one segment and one flow. The time that takes is a better guide to ongoing cost than the feature list. Finally, price the trial at the list size you expect in twelve months, not the size you have today.
        </p>

        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Klaviyo is the better pick for a Shopify store whose main goal is retention, because its profiles, segments and SMS are built around store activity and it offers a free plan for small lists. ActiveCampaign is the better pick for a business that needs deal stages, lead scoring or a CRM alongside its email. Choose based on what the list has to do, and use the pricing pages to check the cost at your real list size.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-activecampaign">Drip vs ActiveCampaign</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-mailchimp">Klaviyo vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip">Klaviyo vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/supplements">Email apps for supplement stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
